//! member: true
//!
//! std.net — blocking TCP over rows: bind a listener, accept or dial a
//! connection, read and write text on it, and close it exactly once.
//! Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **Errors are rows, never traps** (D30, API-CONVENTIONS §2's trap rule
//! and §14's restatement of it for the os tier): a refused connection, a
//! peer that finished, a deadline, bytes that are not text and every other
//! host failure are ORDINARY outcomes a caller handles with `else` or
//! propagates with `?`. Nothing here traps, and nothing returns a
//! sentinel. The vocabulary is the s39 builtin tier's, adopted verbatim so
//! that std adds no translation layer:
//!
//! - `refused` — nobody is listening at that address
//! - `timeout` — the operation's deadline expired (see the deadline note
//!   below: DECLARED so the vocabulary is stable, and not reachable from
//!   wolf source at this pin)
//! - `closed` — the peer finished: the socket's `eof`, an outcome rather
//!   than a failure, and the value that ends a read loop
//! - `utf8` — the bytes read are not well-formed UTF-8; a wolf `str` is
//!   UTF-8 by definition, so a read that cannot decode is a miss
//! - `io` — every other host failure, including a forged, closed or
//!   wrong-kind handle, coarsened to one tag because no caller can act
//!   differently on them yet (§12's rule 3)
//!
//! **Capability: `net` (I13).** Every function here except the two address
//! helpers reaches the host's network stack, is tagged `net` in the
//! toolchain's sandbox table, and is categorically refused at comptime
//! (D33) — sockets are the loudest case of that rule: `wolf add` must
//! never mean arbitrary code talks to the network with the builder's
//! credentials. The refusal is `E0701`, and
//! `tests/net/comptime_refuses.lu` holds it — one level down, at the builtin,
//! because a `comptime fn` cannot call across a module boundary at this pin
//! and so cannot reach this facade at all (F-0051).
//!
//! **Loopback and port 0, always — no external network, EVER.** Every
//! test and every doc example in this module binds `127.0.0.1:0`: the
//! host assigns an ephemeral port and the program learns it through
//! `port`. Nothing here names a fixed port, a foreign host or a name to
//! resolve. `connect` hands its address string to the host, which means a
//! hostname WOULD be resolved by the host resolver — std does not do it,
//! does not test it, and `dns`/`resolve` is not a function this module has
//! (see below).
//!
//! **Two operations sc08 withdrew are HERE now** (sc11): `read_all` and
//! `read_lines`, with `write_line` as the sending half that pairs with
//! them. All three need a loop that stops on `closed` and re-raises
//! `timeout`/`utf8`/`io` unchanged, which needs a handler that can say WHICH
//! tag fired — the shape that matched its first arm for every tag on the
//! executing lane through all of sc08 and sc09 (F-0052, closed at s70;
//! F-0043's payload half at s71). The version this module refused to ship
//! for two sprints would have treated a read error as the end of the stream
//! and handed back a truncated message with no indication, which is what
//! §14's rule about an indistinguishable failure mode exists to prevent.
//!
//! **The three-way split** (§8/§9's rule, applied to sockets). The
//! IMPLEMENTATION owns the builtins this module delegates to
//! (`net_listen`, `net_port`, `net_accept`, `net_connect`, `net_read`,
//! `net_write`, `net_close`) — their semantics are therefore part of this
//! module's contract and are documented per function. std owns everything
//! else: the `Listener` and `Socket` values, the consuming closers, and
//! the pure address helpers. The LANGUAGE owns nothing here; there is no
//! socket syntax.
//!
//! **Blocking, and what that means for a program.** Every operation blocks
//! the whole process: `accept` waits for a connection, `read` waits for
//! bytes. There is no reactor, no task parking and no `select` at this tier
//! — X6's completion-io posture is the s35 reactor's story and reaches std
//! when the builtin tier does. Two consequences a caller must design
//! around: a server written over these functions serves one connection at
//! a time, and an `accept` with no peer coming never returns (which is why
//! every test in this module connects BEFORE it accepts).
//!
//! **What is deliberately absent.** Each of these is a reviewed contract
//! with a filing behind it rather than an approximation:
//!
//! - `set_deadline(mut s, millis)` / `read_timeout` — **the `timeout` tag
//!   is declared and unreachable.** The runtime has a per-socket deadline
//!   (`wolf_rt`'s net table implements one and unit-tests it), and NO
//!   builtin exposes it, so no wolf program can arm one; the executing
//!   lane's sockets are plain blocking sockets that never consult it
//!   (F-0049). std ships no deadline helper rather than one that cannot
//!   work, and every signature already carries the tag so that arming a
//!   deadline is a pure addition when the builtin lands.
//! - `read_line(mut s) -> str ! {closed, timeout, utf8, io}` — ONE line,
//!   as it arrives. Still blocked, and this is now the module's only
//!   blocked read: a buffered reader is writable in principle (a `str`
//!   field mutated through a `mut` parameter works on both executing lanes,
//!   measured in sc08) but FILLING the buffer is a byte-count read, and a
//!   chunk that ends inside a code point is a `utf8` miss whose bytes are
//!   already gone (F-0050). A line reader that corrupts the moment a stream
//!   carries a non-ASCII character is not a partial function, it is a broken
//!   one; §9's "refusal over approximation" decides it. `read_lines` below
//!   is NOT a substitute — it returns nothing until the peer closes — and
//!   the difference is stated on both.
//! - `write_all` — **not shipped because `write` already is one.** The
//!   builtin writes the whole `str` or raises; a short write is not
//!   observable, so a `write_all` loop here would be ceremony documenting
//!   a hazard that does not exist at this pin. The name is reserved for the
//!   day a partial-write surface appears, and `write`'s doc states the
//!   completeness it relies on.
//! - `shutdown(mut s, half)` — an orderly half-close. No builtin, so a
//!   writer cannot say "I am done sending" without closing the whole
//!   socket (F-0049).
//! - `peer_address` / `local_address` — `port` is the only address
//!   information the builtin tier reports, and it reports it as an `int`
//!   for the LOCAL end (F-0049).
//! - UDP (`send_to`/`recv_from`), name resolution, and any HTTP: no
//!   builtin surface at this pin, and the sprint's non-targets besides.
//!   TLS is never this tier's business.
//! - Byte-level `read_bytes`/`write_bytes` — no `net_*` builtin moves
//!   bytes at this pin. This used to be the same gap as `std.fs`'s and it
//!   is not any more: s90 gave the FS tier its byte calls (F-0044 closed,
//!   `fs.copy_file` is a byte copy now) and left the net tier where it
//!   was, so the shape of the fix is known and the socket half is
//!   unwritten (F-0080). It is why `read` carries a `utf8` row at all.
//!
//! **Lanes (re-measured at the sc11 pin).** The compiler's checked tier is
//! the executing lane: it opens REAL sockets (the comptime sandbox is the one place they are refused,
//! D33). lupin has no `net_*` builtins by design — a program that reaches
//! them is an honest `unsupported` at resolve, the conservatism posture the
//! rig records once rather than per test — but lupin resolves module bodies
//! LAZILY, so the two pure address helpers RUN there, which is a property of
//! that lane rather than a leak in this module. The native rung refuses the
//! tier by name ("net
//! builtins in native lowering (checked lane only at s39)"), and refuses
//! the whole module including the pure helpers, because lowering budgets a
//! module by more than the body it needs.

/// A bound TCP listener: the host socket this process accepts connections
/// on.
///
/// The field is an `int` because that is what the builtin tier hands out —
/// a small non-negative index into the machine's socket table, never a
/// pointer. Reading it is legal; passing a hand-built `Listener` to
/// `accept` is not a trap but a miss (`io`), because a forged descriptor is
/// a checkable condition rather than a contract violation, and a public
/// struct's field cannot be private at this pin.
///
/// **Staleness is prevented by the mode system**, exactly as it is for
/// `std.fs.File`: `close_listener` takes its `Listener` by `take` (X1), so
/// using one after closing it is a static use-after-move rather than a
/// row on every operation for a condition the compiler can see.
pub struct Listener {
    fd: int,
}

/// A connected TCP socket: one end of an established stream, whichever
/// side opened it.
///
/// `accept` and `connect` both answer one of these, and nothing about the
/// value records which — the operations are identical once the connection
/// exists, which is the whole point of the type. The field is an `int` for
/// the reasons `Listener`'s is, and `close` consumes the value for the same
/// reason `close_listener` does.
pub struct Socket {
    fd: int,
}

/// Bind a listening socket to `addr` and return it.
///
/// `addr` is `"host:port"` as the host understands it — write
/// `"127.0.0.1:0"` and let the host choose the port, which is what `port`
/// below is for. Rows: `io` when the address cannot be bound at all
/// (already in use, not a local address, malformed), coarsened to one tag
/// because the builtin tier reports one. Never traps.
///
/// The listener starts accepting immediately: a peer may connect before
/// `accept` is ever called and waits in the host's backlog, which is what
/// makes a single-process test deterministic.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_listen`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen(net.loopback(0))?
/// (net.port(srv) else 0) > 0
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn listen(addr: str) -> Listener ! {io} {
    Listener { fd: net_listen(addr)? }
}

/// The port `l` is bound to.
///
/// This is how a program learns the port the host chose for it after
/// `listen(":0")` — the port-0 discipline every test in this module
/// follows. Rows: `io` when the handle is closed, forged or the host
/// cannot report the address. Never traps.
///
/// Read mode: asking a listener its port does not change it. The answer is
/// the LOCAL port only; there is no peer or full-address accessor at this
/// pin (see the module header).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_port`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv) else 0
/// p > 0
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn port(l: Listener) -> int ! {io} {
    net_port(l.fd)?
}

/// Take the next connection from `l`'s backlog, waiting until one arrives.
///
/// Rows: `timeout` when a deadline expires — declared for the vocabulary's
/// sake and not reachable at this pin, because nothing in wolf can arm one
/// (F-0049) — and `io` when the handle is closed, forged or the host
/// failed. Never traps.
///
/// **This blocks until a peer connects.** There is no non-blocking accept
/// and no way to poll, so a program that accepts before anyone dials waits
/// forever; a test connects first and lets the backlog hold the connection
/// (which is exactly what the doc example below does).
///
/// `mut l` because accepting ADVANCES the listener: the connection this
/// call takes is one the next call will not see, which is state the value
/// names even though its `fd` field does not change — the same rule
/// `std.fs.read` follows for a file's position (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_accept`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// net.write(mut cli, "hi")?
/// (net.read(mut conn, 8) else "?") == "hi"
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn accept(mut l: Listener) -> Socket ! {timeout, io} {
    Socket { fd: net_accept(l.fd)? }
}

/// Dial `addr` and return the connected socket.
///
/// Rows: `refused` when nobody is listening there — the commonest network
/// outcome there is, and a row rather than a trap because the caller could
/// not have checked; `timeout` when a deadline expires (declared, not
/// reachable at this pin — F-0049); `io` for every other host failure,
/// including an address that does not parse. Never traps.
///
/// **This blocks until the connection completes or fails**, and the host
/// decides how long that takes. `addr` is handed to the host as written,
/// so a NAME would be resolved by the host resolver — std neither resolves
/// nor tests names, and every example here is loopback (see the module
/// header on the no-external-network law).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_connect`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect(net.loopback(p))?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn connect(addr: str) -> Socket ! {refused, timeout, io} {
    Socket { fd: net_connect(addr)? }
}

/// Read up to `max` bytes from `s`, decoded as UTF-8, waiting for at least
/// one byte.
///
/// Rows: `closed` when the peer finished — the socket's `eof`, an OUTCOME
/// that ends a read loop rather than a failure, and the tag a reader stops
/// on rather than reports; `timeout` when a deadline expires (declared, not reachable
/// — F-0049); `utf8` when the bytes read are not well-formed UTF-8, WHICH
/// INCLUDES a chunk boundary that lands inside a code point, and the bytes
/// are consumed either way; `io` when the handle is closed, forged or the
/// host failed. Never traps.
///
/// **A byte-count read of text is not safe in general**, exactly as
/// `std.fs.read` is not: TCP delivers whatever has arrived, so a stream
/// carrying non-ASCII text can split a code point across two reads and the
/// second byte-sequence is a `utf8` miss. ASCII is safe at any size, and
/// the honest fix is byte-level io (F-0080, and the fs tier has had it
/// since s90 — `fs.read_chunk` is the shape this one wants), filed rather
/// than hidden. `max` at or below zero reads nothing and answers the empty
/// string without touching the socket.
///
/// `mut s` because reading CONSUMES from the stream: the bytes this call
/// takes are bytes the next call will not see (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_read`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "wolfpack")?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read(mut conn, 4) else "?") == "wolf"
/// (net.read(mut conn, 4) else "?") == "pack"
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read(mut s: Socket, max: int) -> str ! {closed, timeout, utf8, io} {
    net_read(s.fd, max)?
}

/// Write all of `text` to `s`.
///
/// Rows: `closed` when the peer has gone away (a write to a finished
/// connection is the row, never a trap), `io` when the handle is closed,
/// forged or the host failed. Never traps, and there is no `utf8` row: a
/// `str` is already well-formed UTF-8.
///
/// **The whole string is written or the call raises** — the builtin loops
/// internally, so a partial write is not an outcome this surface has, and
/// that is why there is no `write_all` beside this function (see the
/// module header). What it does NOT promise is delivery: bytes handed to
/// the host may still be in flight, and only the peer's own read proves
/// otherwise.
///
/// Writes are unbuffered: every call reaches the host, so a program
/// writing a message in small pieces pays for each one.
///
/// `mut s` because writing advances the stream, the same rule `read`
/// follows (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_write`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "one ")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "two")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read(mut conn, 7) else "?") == "one two"
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn write(mut s: Socket, text: str) -> () ! {closed, io} {
    net_write(s.fd, text)?
}

/// Close `s`, consuming it.
///
/// Rows: `io` when the handle was already closed or is foreign. Never
/// traps.
///
/// `take` is the mode of the operation's meaning (§3, and §14's handle
/// rule): closing ends the socket's life, so the caller spells
/// `net.close(take s)` and the compiler rejects every later use as a
/// use-after-move. That is the whole staleness story for sockets, it costs
/// nothing at run time, and `tests/net/use_after_close.lu` holds it as a
/// rejection so it cannot rot.
///
/// Closing is also what tells the PEER the stream is finished: it is the
/// only half-close this tier has, so it is what makes the other end's next
/// read answer `closed`. A writer that wants to say "done sending" and keep
/// reading wants `shutdown`, which is a contract at this pin (F-0049).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_close`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn close(take s: Socket) -> () ! {io} {
    net_close(s.fd)?
}

/// Close the listener `l`, consuming it.
///
/// Rows: `io` when it was already closed or is foreign. Never traps.
///
/// **Why the name is not `close`.** wolf has no overloading, so one module
/// cannot spell one verb for two types (§1: one concept, one name — and
/// the vocabulary word `close` goes to the value a program handles most,
/// the connected socket). Closing a listener stops new connections; the
/// ones already accepted are separate sockets and are unaffected.
///
/// `take` for `close`'s reason: the handle is gone afterwards, and using
/// it is a static use-after-move.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_close`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn close_listener(take l: Listener) -> () ! {io} {
    net_close(l.fd)?
}

/// Every byte the peer sends until it closes, decoded as UTF-8, as one
/// `str`.
///
/// Rows: `timeout` when a deadline expires (declared, not reachable at this
/// pin — F-0049); `utf8` when what arrived is not well-formed UTF-8, WHICH
/// INCLUDES a chunk boundary that lands inside a code point (see below);
/// `io` for a host failure or a bad handle. All three are RE-RAISED
/// unchanged from the read they came from. `closed` is not in the row: the
/// peer finishing is what makes this function RETURN, which is the whole
/// difference between reading everything and failing to read something.
/// Never traps.
///
/// **This blocks until the peer CLOSES**, not until it stops sending. A
/// peer that writes a request and waits for a reply is a peer this function
/// never returns from — so it is the right operation for a stream whose end
/// is the message boundary (a one-shot response, a file transfer) and the
/// wrong one for a conversation. `read` with a limit is the operation for a
/// conversation.
///
/// **The `utf8` hazard is inherited and is real**: the socket is read in
/// 4096-byte chunks and a chunk boundary can split a code point, so a
/// stream carrying non-ASCII text can raise `utf8` even though every byte
/// it sent was valid (F-0050 — the byte-level read that would fix it does
/// not exist). ASCII is safe at any size. The bytes read before the miss
/// are gone either way, which is why this function reports the tag rather
/// than returning a partial answer beside it.
///
/// **Why this function exists only now.** It was written and withdrawn
/// inside sc08: the loop must stop on `closed` and re-raise the other three
/// unchanged, and no handler could tell tags apart — a bare binding catches
/// everything (F-0043) and a `match` inside a handler matched its FIRST ARM
/// for every tag on the executing lane (F-0052). Both are closed at this
/// pin (s70's match tier, s71's payload pattern), so the handler below
/// discriminates on every lane. The version std refused to ship is visible
/// in what it would have done: treated a read error as the end of the
/// stream and handed the caller a truncated message with no indication
/// (§14's rule about a failure mode a module cannot distinguish).
///
/// `mut s` because reading consumes from the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_read`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write(mut cli, "the whole message")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read_all(mut conn) else "?") == "the whole message"
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read_all(mut s: Socket) -> str ! {timeout, utf8, io} {
    var out = ""
    var more = true
    while more {
        let chunk = net_read(s.fd, 4096) else |e| match e {
            closed => {
                more = false
                ""
            },
            timeout => {
                return timeout
            },
            utf8 => {
                return utf8
            },
            io => {
                return io
            },
        }
        if more {
            out += chunk
        }
    }
    out
}

/// Write `text` to `s` followed by exactly one `"\n"`.
///
/// Rows: `write`'s exactly — `closed` when the peer has gone away, `io` for
/// a host failure or a bad handle. Never traps, and there is no `utf8` row:
/// a `str` is already well-formed UTF-8.
///
/// This is the SENDING half of a line protocol, and it is one call rather
/// than two so that a message and its terminator cannot be separated by an
/// early return between them. The terminator is one `"\n"` on every
/// platform — no `"\r\n"` translation anywhere, matching `std.io`'s
/// writers, because a protocol that wants the other terminator can write it
/// with `write` and a program should never have to guess which one it got.
///
/// `mut s` because writing advances the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_write`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write_line(mut cli, "GET /")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// (net.read_all(mut conn) else "?") == "GET /\n"
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn write_line(mut s: Socket, text: str) -> () ! {closed, io} {
    net_write(s.fd, "{text}\n")?
}

/// Every line the peer sends until it closes, terminators stripped.
///
/// Rows: `read_all`'s exactly — `timeout` (declared, unreachable at this
/// pin), `utf8` (including a chunk boundary inside a code point), `io` —
/// and `closed` is again the OUTCOME that ends the read rather than a tag.
/// Never traps. Allocates the list.
///
/// This is the RECEIVING half of a line protocol, and its shape is the
/// honest one available at this pin: read the whole stream, then split it.
/// The alternative — a `read_line` that returns ONE line as it arrives —
/// needs a buffer filled by a byte-count read, and a chunk that ends inside
/// a code point loses those bytes with no way to report which line they
/// belonged to (F-0050). That function is a reviewed contract in the module
/// header, and this one is not a substitute for it: a caller who must react
/// to each line as it arrives cannot use this, because nothing is returned
/// until the peer closes.
///
/// A trailing terminator produces no final empty line (`"a\nb\n"` is two
/// lines, not three), the empty stream is an empty list, and the split is
/// `std.str.lines`'s — Unicode line boundaries, which is the builtin's
/// definition and therefore part of this contract.
///
/// `mut s` because reading consumes from the stream (§14).
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `net_read`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var srv = net.listen("127.0.0.1:0")?
/// let p = net.port(srv)?
/// var cli = net.connect("127.0.0.1:{p}")?
/// net.write_line(mut cli, "one")?
/// net.write_line(mut cli, "two")?
/// net.close(take cli)?
/// var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
/// let got = net.read_lines(mut conn) else List[str]()
/// got.len == 2
/// net.close(take conn)?
/// net.close_listener(take srv)?
/// ```
pub fn read_lines(mut s: Socket) -> List[str] ! {timeout, utf8, io} {
    let whole = read_all(mut s)?
    whole.lines()
}

/// `host` and `port_number` joined as the address string `listen` and
/// `connect` take.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps — and pure, so unlike everything above it is
/// comptime-safe and reaches no host, which is why it is the one function
/// here a `comptime fn` may call. An IPv6 host (one containing a `:`) is
/// bracketed, because that is what makes `"::1"` and a port
/// unambiguous; anything else is joined with a single `:`. Nothing is
/// validated: an address the host cannot parse comes back as an `io` row
/// from `listen` or `connect`, where the host is the authority.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// net.endpoint("127.0.0.1", 8080) == "127.0.0.1:8080"
/// net.endpoint("::1", 8080) == "[::1]:8080"
/// net.endpoint("localhost", 0) == "localhost:0"
/// ```
pub fn endpoint(host: str, port_number: int) -> str {
    if host.contains(":") { return "[{host}]:{port_number}" }
    "{host}:{port_number}"
}

/// The loopback address with `port_number` — `endpoint("127.0.0.1", n)`.
///
/// Total, pure, comptime-safe. It exists to make the discipline this
/// module tests itself under writable in one call: `loopback(0)` asks the
/// host for an ephemeral port on the interface that cannot leave the
/// machine, which is the only address a std test or doc example is ever
/// allowed to name.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// net.loopback(0) == "127.0.0.1:0"
/// net.loopback(8080) == "127.0.0.1:8080"
/// ```
pub fn loopback(port_number: int) -> str {
    endpoint("127.0.0.1", port_number)
}
